00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.810
So that we're all on the same
page, what is case-routing?

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.390
So case-routing is whenever
you have a department, a team,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.680
or even a single person
that is in charge

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.110
of taking either
external queries

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:51.956
or questions or internal
ones, and routing

00:03:51.956 --> 00:03:53.080
them-- case-routing, right?

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.760
Routing them to where
they need to go.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.770
In some cases, they can
even answer them, right?

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:02.570
And show of hands, who's
been on-call for their job?

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:03.070
Yeah.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:03.640
I was on-call.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:05.140
When I was in health
care, I was on-call,

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.580
and it was a really tough job.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:07.480
Very stressful.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.730
Because I remember,
health care never sleeps.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.790
Machines go down on a
weekend or at night,

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.589
and I didn't know who to
turn to, where to get help.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.130
The people I would
normally ask help,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.171
they were either sleeping
or playing golf, right?

00:04:20.171 --> 00:04:22.089
So it was very
stressful, and that's

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:26.374
why I have a personal agenda to
work on this to fix this issue.

00:04:26.374 --> 00:04:28.040
And why would you
want to automate this?

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.414
Well, the obvious
reasons, right?

00:04:29.414 --> 00:04:30.970
You're removing
that middle, layer--

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.950
the people on-call like me
who don't want to be on-call.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:34.100
You're removing that.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.809
And the customer or the
client or the employee

00:04:36.809 --> 00:04:38.350
who has a question--
they know that's

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.470
going to go to the right
department immediately.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:42.582
You get 24/7 support.

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.290
You can handle multiple
languages, right?

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.539
We modeled this in English, but
you could very well model it

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:48.580
in any language you want.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.038
So once you have
one language down,

00:04:50.038 --> 00:04:52.600
it's just a question of
getting enough historical data

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.940
to model other languages.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.500
You can do Auto Reply, right?

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.180
Very simple Auto Reply.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.472
Somebody's saying, when
is the office open?

00:05:00.472 --> 00:05:02.680
And you say, oh, this country
has a national holiday.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.250
Try this date, right?

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:05.350
These are easy wins.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.300
You could automate that.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.120
And more important, I think,
is a centralized digital trail.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:14.350
You're going to start getting a
warehouse of all your queries,

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.940
all your questions.

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.010
So who's asking what
type of question?

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.210
Where are their problems?

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.580
What department
tackles those answers?

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.767
Which one are struggling
to answer those questions?

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:25.350
All that is gold for
a company, right?

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.190
You can really learn a
lot about your company,

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:28.773
about what's going
on with your costs,

00:05:28.773 --> 00:05:31.550
and learning about
your customers as well.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.537
So these are the
three main models

00:05:33.537 --> 00:05:34.870
that we're going to talk about--

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.767
basically, the brains behind
what we're going to present.

00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:39.850
There's going to be a
convolutional neural network

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.450
that's going to do
the predicting of what

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.000
department to route a case to.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.270
There is the Google
Language API that we're

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.280
going to leverage as much
information as we can

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.680
from there to get
additional information.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.769
And finally, finding
similar cases.

00:05:56.769 --> 00:05:58.060
So you got a case that came in.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.950
You want to know, what
is a similar case,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:01.100
and how was it resolved?

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:04.680
That's great hints for somebody
who's handling the case load.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.070
And as a high-level
architecture,

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.702
before we dive
into the CNNs, this

00:06:08.702 --> 00:06:09.910
is what we build in the path.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.535
This is what we would
build today, too.

00:06:11.535 --> 00:06:13.600
You have the incoming
case that comes in,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.890
and you have the App Engine.

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.750
So the App Engine is
a serverless instance

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:18.320
in the Cloud.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.350
It's a great type of instance--

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.250
Compute Engine-- for
this type of project,

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.520
especially if you have
cyclical case loads, right?

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.740
So if you have more
cases during the year,

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.130
these things will size
automatically, will balance,

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:32.210
will scale.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.220
And you pay only
for what you use.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.390
It's also the choreographer,
the manager of the data.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:42.200
It will do some data cleaning,
pass it to the Cloud MLE--

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.440
the mounted Machine Learning
Engine in the Cloud--

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.970
which is hosting our
convolutional neural network.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:49.970
And Priya will talk
more about the data set,

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.900
but it's about financial
consumer complaints, right?

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.890
We're using an open
source data set,

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.770
and it can route it into
one of seven departments--

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.357
Consumer Loan, Vehicle Loan,
Bank Account, Student Loan,

00:07:00.357 --> 00:07:01.940
Mortgage, Credit
Card, or Payday Loan.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.280
So these departments
are all related

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.380
and all within the
financial industry.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:10.640
And so the CNN will tell you
what department to route it to.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.105
So now we know
where to route it.

00:07:13.105 --> 00:07:15.230
The App Engine will then
also pass that information

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:16.430
to the Language API,
where we're going

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.721
to get all sorts of information
we can, like sentiment.

00:07:18.721 --> 00:07:21.380
We'll know the tone of the case.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:24.380
Things like the
organization it involves.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:28.730
And now we can populate that
information in our case report.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.900
And we're also going
to query BigQuery--

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:36.380
a huge, bottomless data
warehouse where, I was reading,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.460
it could query 35 billion rows
in seconds, with no indexes.

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.460
So a very powerful tool.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:45.390
Perfect for storing all of your
knowledge base, all your cases.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.390
And here we're going
to pull similar cases.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:51.800
And the end-product for the
demo is this smart report,

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:52.550
we call it, right?

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.130
So it will tell you what
the complaint is, give you

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.320
some hints about
what's going on there,

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:00.289
and some suggestions
of how to resolve it.

00:08:00.289 --> 00:08:01.580
So this is for the demo, right?

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.500
Most of the cases, we
would feed this directly

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:04.874
to the customer's CRM, right?

00:08:04.874 --> 00:08:06.290
They probably have
a tool already.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.235
So either a rest point
or an API, right?

00:08:08.235 --> 00:08:09.860
But for the sake of
the demo, we wanted

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:11.630
something that was visual.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:14.290
And I'm going to pass
it to Priya, who's

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.020
going to talk about the CNNs.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.270
PRIYA DWIVEDI: Thanks, Manuel.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:20.390
So I'm going to talk about how
we build a Convolutional Neural

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:23.550
Network in TensorFlow
to do the case-routing.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.810
So before I start,
by show of hands,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:29.120
can I know how many of you
have built any kind of model

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.550
in TensorFlow before?

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:32.309
OK, that's a lot.

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:33.980
That's really good to know.

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:36.510
I've kept the talk
here high level,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.640
but if you have any
specific questions,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.980
feel free to stop
me after the talk.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.980
So the first thing
I want to talk about

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:48.310
is the data set that we
use to build this model.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.470
Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau in the US

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.180
has a publicly
available data set

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:56.710
of 274,000 consumer complaints
across many different products

00:08:56.710 --> 00:09:00.800
like mortgage, credit cards,
student loans, et cetera.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.100
When a consumer
complains to CFPB,

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.860
they have the option of
making their complaint public.

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:08.750
And when they decide to do that,
any sensitive information--

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.750
like their names or amounts
or dates-- are anonymous,

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:14.800
and this data is
publicly available.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.500
We think this is a really good
data set for deep learning,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.210
because it's pretty big.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.010
There's a huge variety here.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:23.320
It uses natural language.

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.070
And what I mean by
that is if you actually

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:26.528
read through the
complaints, you'll

00:09:26.528 --> 00:09:28.390
see that some complaints
are very long.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:29.110
Some are short.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:30.310
There are spelling mistakes.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:31.950
Some consumers
repeat themselves.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:35.480
So it's perfect to use
that for deep learning.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:39.390
I also feel that the database
is very rich in financial terms,

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.600
so it's possible to actually
use this dataset for other use

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.300
cases, like routing
customer service calls.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.340
So now that we have
the dataset, you

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:55.550
can think of this dataset
as individual complaints,

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.320
and complaints can
be long or short.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.870
So I've taken a
sample complaint here

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:02.120
that says, "You declined
my transaction when

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:02.900
I was shopping."

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.420
And this is all words,
but neural networks

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.050
love numbers and math, so we
do pre-processing that converts

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:12.270
these sentences into numbers.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:15.230
The first thing we do
is we break the sentence

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.280
into individual words.

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.770
And so that would
be "You" "declined"

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:21.320
"my" "transaction," et cetera.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.240
And then we map these
words into integers.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.420
So we could say that
"You" corresponds to 23

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.550
and "declined" corresponds
to 45 and so on,

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.725
and this can be arbitrary.

00:10:30.725 --> 00:10:31.850
You can choose any of them.

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.410
You just need to
save this mapping

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.645
for later so if you want to
convert the integers back

00:10:35.645 --> 00:10:38.280
to words, you can do that.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.620
So now we have our complaints
represented as integers.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.920
So when we feed them
into the neural network,

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.290
we have to assign a
tensor to hold them.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.210
And TensorFlow likes
the tensors to be fixed

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.420
sized so you can
specify them in advance.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.050
So this means that it
will like all complaints

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:58.250
to be of the same length.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.339
But in your data set,
different complaints

00:11:00.339 --> 00:11:01.380
can be different lengths.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:04.163
So to solve this,
we choose a length,

00:11:04.163 --> 00:11:06.162
and then we make sure
everything is that length.

00:11:06.162 --> 00:11:08.050
So if it's longer than
that, we truncate it.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:10.080
If it's shorter than
that, then we pad it.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.870
So let's say, in this
hypothetical example,

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:15.360
we want everything
to be 12 words long.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.240
So this is four
short, so we add four

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:22.460
0's at the end, which correspond
to a pad token in the model.

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:26.130
But just for reference, the
actual dataset was much longer,

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:31.010
so the complaints were
padded to 600 words.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:33.500
OK, so awesome.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:38.240
After this point, our dataset is
all complaints of same length,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.150
all of them represented
by integers.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.880
But if you think about it,
all these integers are unique.

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:46.370
So we have all words
represented uniquely,

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:48.470
but that's not really
how our language is.

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.747
Words are not unique.

00:11:49.747 --> 00:11:51.080
Words are related to each other.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.310
Some words are synonyms.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:53.720
Some are antonyms.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:57.480
Some words are very likely
to be in similar contexts.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:58.910
For example, a
king and queen are

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:01.760
very likely to be talked
about in the same context,

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.670
much more so than
king and AI, right?

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.250
So to get over that,
or to give the model

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:10.070
our understanding
of English language,

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:13.560
we convert each
word into a vector.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.380
And this can be a
vector of any size.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:18.620
We ended up using 128
dimensional vector.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:22.070
And so what happens is that
words that are similar,

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.950
are likely to occur
in similar contexts,

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.720
have word vectors
that are closer

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.920
to each other in
the vector space.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.850
And so this allows
us to give the model

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.750
understanding of semantics.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.920
So to train these word vectors,
we can use two techniques.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:39.949
We can train these
word vectors as part

00:12:39.949 --> 00:12:41.240
of training the neural network.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:44.130
So we initialize them
randomly, and then we

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:48.050
use backpropagation to train
them as we build the model.

00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:49.850
And that's what we
did in this case.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:53.000
And this allows the vectors
to have understanding

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.440
of financial terms.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.300
The other way is
that you can actually

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.120
use pre-trained word
vectors, and these

00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:00.020
are available on the internet.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.624
I think GloVe is a good source
of these pre-trained vectors.

00:13:02.624 --> 00:13:04.040
And these pre-trained
vectors have

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.920
been trained on huge
corpus of English language,

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.650
like Wikipedia and
stuff, so they are also

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:13.340
good at generally understanding
what context words are used in.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.095
So Manuel will later
show a use case

00:13:15.095 --> 00:13:17.680
where he used the
pre-trained word vectors.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.920
OK, so now took our complaint
and we converted everything

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.280
into numbers, so we're good
to use a neural network.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.690
So in this case, we build a
CNN for text classification.

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:32.260
I think a lot of you
might have heard of CNNs.

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.060
They have been quite
popular in computer vision.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:36.980
They have been used
very successfully

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.810
on images for detection,
tracking, classification, et

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:40.310
cetera.

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:42.800
But more recently,
CNNs have actually

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.980
been used very successfully for
text classification as well,

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.980
and this might sound a
little bit unintuitive.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:50.750
So part of the goal
of this slide for me

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:53.070
is to actually explain
to you the architecture

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:55.460
on a high level, and
also give some intuition

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:57.120
around why this actually works.

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.980
So maybe let's start with
a small sentence here.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.360
"I like this movie very much."

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:05.450
So this is seven
words, and each word

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:08.000
is represented by a
five-dimensional word vector.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.700
So you can think of
this as a 7 by 5 metric.

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:14.030
And at this point, you can see
it's somehow like an image,

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:15.890
because it becomes 2D.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.840
And then we choose
different filters.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:20.802
So we choose filters of
size two words, three words,

00:14:20.802 --> 00:14:23.260
and four words, and they're
represented by different colors

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:24.770
here.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:27.650
These filters will do
the convolution operation

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:29.570
on the sentence.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:33.110
One way of thinking about this
convolution operation is--

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:35.660
so filter of two words will
look at two adjacent words.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:37.580
Three words will
look at three words,

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:38.960
and then four
would look at four.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:40.950
And so this is a bag of words.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.640
So the neural network is trying
to look at adjacent words

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:46.786
and trying to extract
meaning from the sentence.

00:14:46.786 --> 00:14:48.410
And then it creates
these feature maps,

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:51.430
which are these
one-dimensional vectors that

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:52.790
have summarized everything.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:54.790
And you can see them
as different vectors

00:14:54.790 --> 00:14:57.380
of different colors here.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.440
And then finally, since
all of these feature maps

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:02.500
are different length,
to actually use them

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:04.810
in the neural network,
we do a hacky fix

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:07.020
where we do a 1D max pooling.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:10.660
We take the maximum value from
each of these feature maps

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:15.460
and combine them to create
this colored vector at the end.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:17.590
So the way I think of
all of this operation

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:21.220
is the neural network has-- the
CNN has gone over the sentence

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.409
and extracted all the
relevant information

00:15:23.409 --> 00:15:25.450
and combined that into
that colored vector, which

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:28.620
is the output from this stage.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.730
So I want to summarize the model
once more before I move on.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:35.440
So we started with raw
text of complaints.

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:37.900
We did pre-processing
that converted the text

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:39.250
into integers.

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:42.670
Then we converted all the word
integers into word vectors.

00:15:42.670 --> 00:15:44.380
We built the CNN.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:47.020
In our actual CNN, we use
four different filter sizes

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.600
of two words, three words,
four words, and five words.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.329
We do the concatenation.

00:15:51.329 --> 00:15:52.870
And then the final
layer in the model

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:56.590
is a fully-connected layer
with sigmoid activation.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:16:00.460
And what this does
is it assigns--

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.884
it basically assigns
each complaint

00:16:02.884 --> 00:16:04.300
into one of the
seven departments,

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:05.710
assigning them
different probabilities.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:07.300
And we can just
pick the department

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.690
with the highest probability as
the prediction from the model.

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:13.870
So as you can see, this is a
relatively simple architecture,

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:18.050
and it's amazing
how well this works.

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:21.740
So we were able to get
85% accuracy in correctly

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.330
predicting department
on the test set, which

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.140
is the set that the
model wasn't exposed

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.290
to at the time of training.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:32.510
Other big benefits
here were the model

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.010
is actually quite
quick to train.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:37.010
So it took only about 15
minutes of training time

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:38.900
on a single GPU.

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:41.180
Since it's so quick to
train, it's very easy

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:44.120
to do tuning of
hyperparameters or testing

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:45.986
on different architectures.

00:16:45.986 --> 00:16:47.902
It runs pretty fast at
inference time as well,

00:16:47.902 --> 00:16:50.690
so we're available to use
a CPU at inference time,

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:54.770
and that's what we will show
in the demo as well, right now.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:57.352
It can also be
re-trained periodically.

00:16:57.352 --> 00:16:59.060
So let's say you roll
out with this model

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:02.120
and you use it for three months
and you collect new real data

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:04.339
in the field, you can
easily plant that back in

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:06.599
and re-train the model.

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:08.849
And then finally, I think
a more subtle but actually

00:17:08.849 --> 00:17:11.180
pretty important point is
because this model uses

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.040
deep learning, you don't really
need to do any complex feature

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:14.540
engineering.

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.920
The model itself
extracts features,

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:18.589
which means that
you don't need to be

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:20.680
an expert in financial
services, and you also

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:23.710
don't need to get an
LP expert to use this.

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:26.800
This, in my opinion, makes
deep learning very versatile.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:29.810
Same architecture can be
applied on different problems

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:30.960
and different domains--

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:34.310
legal and financial
services, wherever

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:37.330
you want to apply them.

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:40.680
OK, so we have talked
a lot about the model,

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:44.170
so now I want to
actually do a demo of it.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:46.680
Let's see if I can
switch screens.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:47.830
OK, this is awesome.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:50.590
So what we did is we
designed a web demo.

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:53.620
So this demo allows us to
interact with the model

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:55.414
more easily.

00:17:55.414 --> 00:17:56.830
So in this first
page of the demo,

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:59.630
we have the customer's
name, phone number,

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.970
and address, which is
arbitrary at this point,

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:05.560
because the actual data had
this information anonymized.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:07.300
And then in this
section right here

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.150
is where we can
put in a complaint,

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:12.730
and then when we run
it, this complaint

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:16.557
is run against the
CNN at the back.

00:18:16.557 --> 00:18:18.890
So the complaint that I've
chosen is an interesting one.

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:22.810
It says, "I'm in the process of
refinancing with Quicken Loans.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:24.820
It's been over 45
days, and I still

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:26.512
don't have a closing date.

00:18:26.512 --> 00:18:27.970
They keep giving
me the run around,

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:30.550
and it's been nothing
but a headache."

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:32.359
So what I personally
find interesting

00:18:32.359 --> 00:18:34.900
about this complaint is there
is no mention of any department

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:37.879
here, and it's actually a
very small complaint as well.

00:18:37.879 --> 00:18:40.420
So if you're not familiar with
the financial services domain,

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:42.640
it might be hard for
you to know what product

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.260
is being talked about here.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:47.620
And that, I think, is the whole
benefit of deep learning--

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:50.320
is just by looking at a
whole bunch of complaints,

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:53.410
it has understood what
are the key terms involved

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:55.970
with each product, and
it's then able to use that.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:02.400
So now when I hit submit,
the neutral network

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:06.300
runs in the backend and
actually assigns this

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:09.580
to the Mortgage Department,
which is correct.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.480
So besides actually predicting
the department, which

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.360
is what the case-routing
would help with,

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.090
we also have, here, what
we call the smart report.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:21.150
So this report has a lot
of interesting insights.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.000
So from the text
in the complaint,

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.040
we are able to create
this word cloud, which has

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.780
the key words in the complaint.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.870
We are also able to
give a sentiment index--

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.210
sentiment score--
to this complaint.

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.180
We can extract
organizations and categories

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:38.160
that are being talked about.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:42.270
As well as we are able to look
into our historical database

00:19:42.270 --> 00:19:44.700
for other similar
complaints and present that

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:47.869
to the agent who's actually
working this complaint.

00:19:47.869 --> 00:19:49.410
And that can be
pretty useful, right?

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:52.080
So if you're working
on this complaint,

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:54.930
you might want to know
what was done in the past,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:58.330
and you can apply a
similar resolution.

00:19:58.330 --> 00:20:00.560
So in the next part
of this presentation,

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:04.150
we want to go and talk
about how we actually

00:20:04.150 --> 00:20:06.060
created this smart report.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:07.340
Switch back.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:09.370
OK, so I'm going to
now talk about how

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:11.560
we use the Google Language API.

00:20:11.560 --> 00:20:13.790
So before I start,
by show of hands,

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:17.050
can I know how many of you
have used the Google Language

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:19.450
API before?

00:20:19.450 --> 00:20:20.820
OK, that's good to know.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:23.370
So those of you who
haven't, I would actually

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.870
encourage you to try it out.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.270
It's pretty simple to use.

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.380
So the home page for
the Natural Language API

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.870
has a section where
you can use it easily.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:38.112
You don't really need to
code in Python to do this.

00:20:38.112 --> 00:20:39.570
So that's what I
want to do now, is

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:43.680
I want to actually
showcase the web page where

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.370
you can use the Language API.

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:49.770
So I clicked right into and went
into the Natural Language API

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:50.290
home page.

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:53.640
And within here, there is
the option to try the API.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:55.650
So I picked a
sample sentence that

00:20:55.650 --> 00:21:00.210
says, "ABC Bank has associated
my number with someone else's

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.490
delinquent account.

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:03.240
They're calling me
every hour and will not

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.520
remove my number from the
other person's account."

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:09.120
So this person is
clearly stressed.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:12.900
So when I hit Analyze,
essentially what would happen

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:16.470
is that the Language
API would go and extract

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:20.660
information and share that
with me across four tabs.

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:25.170
So there's Entity, Sentiment,
Syntax, and Categories.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:28.830
The Entities portion of the
API looks at things like,

00:21:28.830 --> 00:21:30.720
hey, what's an organization?

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:31.630
What's a person?

00:21:31.630 --> 00:21:33.270
What's an event?

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:37.240
And stuff like that and
presents that information.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:41.850
The Sentiment tab gives
me a sentiment score

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:45.960
for the entire text, as well as
for each individual sentence.

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:49.260
So it has correctly
assigned this sentence

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:52.270
a slightly negative
sentiment, which is true.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.300
And then for each
individual sentence,

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:55.530
there's a sentiment score.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.120
What I find very,
very interesting

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.640
here is that if you look at
the second sentence, which

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:01.140
says, "They're
calling me every hour

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.348
and will not remove my number
from the other person's

00:22:03.348 --> 00:22:06.750
account," there's actually no
real big negative word here,

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:08.670
but the model has
correctly understood

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.814
that the overall sentiment
here is pretty negative.

00:22:10.814 --> 00:22:12.480
And I would have
actually done the same.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:15.300
I would have assigned this
sentence a pretty negative

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:16.020
sentiment score.

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:22.230
So the API also has very, very
rich information about syntax.

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.480
So this has things
like part of speech.

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:27.930
What words are noun,
verbs, adverbs, et cetera?

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:30.114
It also has
dependency maps, which

00:22:30.114 --> 00:22:32.530
can show you visually how words
are related to each other.

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:35.850
So you can use this to
very quickly understand

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.560
what are the verbs
in your sentence,

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:41.550
and then do analysis after that.

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:44.300
And then finally, it
also has categories.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:46.470
So for us, it's very generic.

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.050
It says, these complaints are
related to financial services

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:50.460
and banking, which is true.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:52.815
But I think this feature can
be very useful if your data

00:22:52.815 --> 00:22:55.320
set has complaints of
many different types,

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:57.360
and then it can
actually tell you

00:22:57.360 --> 00:22:59.790
what the relevant categories
are quite accurately,

00:22:59.790 --> 00:23:02.200
and will be useful.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.740
OK, so now I want
to hand it back over

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.920
to Manuel, who will walk you
through how we use the Language

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.200
API in our demo.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.940
MANUEL AMUNATEGUI:
Thank you, Priya.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:16.300
So Priya gave us a good overview
of what the Google Language

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:19.510
API is all about, and I want
to dive a little bit deeper

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:21.790
into how we used it
in the smart report.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:25.470
It's a very powerful tool.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:28.550
I think these are pre-trained
models in the Cloud.

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.790
So whenever you can, use
anything that's pre-trained--

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:34.055
whether an image modeling,
transfer learning,

00:23:34.055 --> 00:23:36.800
and using pre-trained
word vectors in NLP--

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:41.640
or tools like these
pre-trained models.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:43.640
Google offers many of
them, not just a language.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.390
There's vision.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:45.260
There's video.

00:23:45.260 --> 00:23:46.169
There's translation.

00:23:46.169 --> 00:23:47.085
There's transcription.

00:23:47.085 --> 00:23:48.482
All sorts of really cool tools.

00:23:48.482 --> 00:23:50.690
Whenever you can push that
work onto other people who

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:52.400
specialize on that, it's a win.

00:23:52.400 --> 00:23:56.870
That allows you to focus you
and your team on the business

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:59.660
use case and leverage these
tools that work really well--

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:03.320
and are probably harder for
us to replicate as well.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:06.680
And it frees up a lot
of time on our end.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.320
So the smart case report--
this portion of the report

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:14.592
is fueled, is powered
by the Language API.

00:24:14.592 --> 00:24:16.550
And I'm going to walk
you through a few things.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:18.920
So the word cloud is
interesting, right?

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:20.840
It's been used a lot
in educational tools,

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:22.370
but it's also very
powerful in terms

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:25.250
of highlighting word
frequencies-- which words are

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:25.970
used the most.

00:24:25.970 --> 00:24:26.870
It's very visual.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:28.520
You don't have to worry too much
about the way it's presented.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:29.894
It's going to
present it for you.

00:24:29.894 --> 00:24:33.620
It will show the largest
words in a larger font

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:34.980
and everything else smaller.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:36.480
But it doesn't have
to be frequency.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:37.490
It can be anything
you want, right?

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:38.540
You can program this.

00:24:38.540 --> 00:24:40.740
You could do, for
example, part of speech,

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:42.920
which Priya was showing
us, by the action verbs.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:44.920
"I want," "I need."

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:47.420
That would be, also, useful in
terms of quickly figuring out

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:50.819
what a piece of text is about.

00:24:50.819 --> 00:24:52.110
They're very easy to implement.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.110
You can program them in
all sorts of languages--

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:56.060
through REST calls,
through Python.

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:58.640
It's just a call to the API.

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:00.570
Oh, and also, the
sentiment index, right?

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.260
It's a number between, I
think, negative 1 and 1, right?

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:07.430
And here we present it in a
linear scale with emoticons

00:25:07.430 --> 00:25:10.330
from very unhappy to very happy.

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:12.006
In a fraction of a
second, you quickly

00:25:12.006 --> 00:25:13.130
know the tone of the email.

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:17.440
So very powerful, especially if
you're doing customer service.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:21.310
The topics-- so those are also
referred to as categories.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:23.226
There is a category
and a subcategory.

00:25:23.226 --> 00:25:24.850
Currently there's,
I think, last time I

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:26.800
read, about 700
categories it supports.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:28.717
And the financial world
does a really good job

00:25:28.717 --> 00:25:30.466
at differentiating it,
because as you saw,

00:25:30.466 --> 00:25:32.620
our seven departments are
pretty closely related.

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:35.140
It still tells the difference
between all sorts of things,

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:36.220
very powerful.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:40.080
And a nice thing about using
these Cloud-based, pre-trained

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.010
models is it's 700 right now.

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:44.510
And you can go to sleep and
when you wake up in the morning,

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:47.094
suddenly it's 1,000 categories,
or 10,000 categories, right?

00:25:47.094 --> 00:25:49.510
So you automatically benefit
from better pre-trained model

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:51.718
without having to worry
about it, because you're just

00:25:51.718 --> 00:25:53.680
calling the API.

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:55.250
The organization's
also really cool.

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:57.960
It will pull all
sorts of the entities.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:04.279
It can pull famous
locations, I think locations;

00:26:04.279 --> 00:26:05.445
in this case, organizations.

00:26:05.445 --> 00:26:08.470
So really smart to pull
these kind of things

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:11.590
and quickly tell the
person or the agent that's

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:13.900
taking care of this, who
are the major players

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.880
in this complaint?

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:20.477
And now I want to talk about
pulling similar complaints that

00:26:20.477 --> 00:26:21.560
were resolved in the past.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:24.010
So there are different ways
you can approach this problem.

00:26:24.010 --> 00:26:26.300
We thought we had a
pretty clever one here.

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:28.660
And the idea is that you
have an incoming case--

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:31.600
an incoming complaint,
for this dataset--

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:36.370
and you want to know what have
other agents done in the past,

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:37.990
and how have they
resolved it, right?

00:26:37.990 --> 00:26:40.670
So not only is that going to
be a lot of help for the agent,

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:43.780
it's also a step closer to
doing auto replies, right?

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.480
Once you know how this works
well, you can determine-- well,

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:50.210
I think the machine leaning
can handle this automatically.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:52.750
Let's just fire off a reply.

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:54.950
So you have this original
complaint that comes.

00:26:54.950 --> 00:26:58.710
Let's imagine it
has 20 words, right?

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:00.730
So you have these 20 words.

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:02.820
It's hard to fish in
the entire knowledge

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.017
base of historical
complaints that we've

00:27:05.017 --> 00:27:07.350
dealt with in the past and
finding a similar one, right?

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:08.010
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:10.470
going to use pre-trained,
word-embedded vectors to boost

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:11.880
the vocabulary, right?

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.560
So Priya talked a bit about it.

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:17.310
What is a pre-trained,
word-embedded vectors?

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:20.160
So GloVe is a
really popular one.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.710
They have taken either
Wikipedia as a corpus

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:24.690
of the English language,
or even Common Crawl,

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:27.300
which is a certain percentage
of the entire internet,

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:30.570
and modeled it to figure out
exactly how each word relates

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:31.860
to each other word.

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.532
So in the end, it's
very complex modeling.

00:27:34.532 --> 00:27:36.990
So that's why we're very happy
somebody else did it for us.

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:38.790
And in the end,
you have a matrix

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:41.910
with all the most commonly
spoken English words,

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.680
and a long array of numbers.

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:47.640
And these numbers for each word
represent how one word relates

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.430
to another, and that's how
you can get the king to queen

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:51.967
is a man to woman.

00:27:51.967 --> 00:27:52.800
It understands that.

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:54.770
There's basically a
mathematical underlying

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:56.850
to the English language
that's captured

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:59.070
in these pre-trained
word vectors.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:00.870
And if anybody has
worked with Word2vec

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:03.992
and has pre-trained their
own corpuses, it's not easy.

00:28:03.992 --> 00:28:04.950
It takes a lot of time.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:06.450
You got to have the
right equipment.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:08.490
And you've got to tune
it correctly, right?

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:10.448
Otherwise you got to go
back and do everything.

00:28:10.448 --> 00:28:13.445
I did that a lot in health
care, and it was a lot of work.

00:28:13.445 --> 00:28:15.570
And we didn't have GPUs,
so it was sometimes a week

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:16.754
to two weeks of work, right?

00:28:16.754 --> 00:28:18.420
Just to find out that
you made a mistake

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:19.586
and you have to do it again.

00:28:19.586 --> 00:28:20.710
Very frustrating.

00:28:20.710 --> 00:28:23.520
So this is phenomenal that
they make these available.

00:28:23.520 --> 00:28:24.670
GloVe is from Stanford.

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:26.670
I think Stanford and GloVe
are working together.

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:28.850
Stanford's maintaining
the GloVe datasets.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:29.880
And they're open source.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:31.680
Anybody can use them.

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:33.180
So what we're doing,
we're basically

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:35.910
taking every word of
that original complaint--

00:28:35.910 --> 00:28:37.480
let's pretend there's 20 words.

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:40.800
And we'll zoom into one
word-- the word "transaction."

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.940
So then you can say, using
this pre-trained matrix,

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:48.180
find me all the words
that are close to the word

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:49.330
"transaction."

00:28:49.330 --> 00:28:51.900
And because you have
this complex matrix that

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:54.950
represents how each word
relates to every other word,

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:56.700
you can use any distance
metric you want--

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:58.890
Euclidean distance,
cosine distance.

00:28:58.890 --> 00:28:59.700
It doesn't matter.

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:03.255
And basically, you've created
an electronic, very powerful

00:29:03.255 --> 00:29:04.499
thesaurus, right?

00:29:04.499 --> 00:29:06.540
So instead of having just
the word "transaction,"

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.914
you can boost it to buying,
business, contract agreement,

00:29:08.914 --> 00:29:10.255
or even hundreds of words.

00:29:10.255 --> 00:29:12.130
So you do that for all
your important words--

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:15.080
not just ignore your stop
words, all your important words.

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:17.370
So you take that case that
has 20 words originally,

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:19.920
and now you can have
10,000 words, right?

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:20.920
And you can go fishing--

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:22.503
basically a big bag
of words-- and you

00:29:22.503 --> 00:29:24.210
can go fishing into
your knowledge base,

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:28.050
and finding the most related,
the closely related cases

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.490
to bring to the surface.

00:29:30.490 --> 00:29:32.199
Of course, you've got
to weigh the words.

00:29:32.199 --> 00:29:34.031
You got to do a little
bit of normalization,

00:29:34.031 --> 00:29:35.850
otherwise the very
large historical cases

00:29:35.850 --> 00:29:37.800
would take precedence, and
you don't want that, right?

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:39.670
So there's a little
bit more work involved,

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:42.840
but that's the overall idea.

00:29:42.840 --> 00:29:43.879
So then you basically--

00:29:43.879 --> 00:29:45.420
I'm going to repeat
what I just said.

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:47.760
You're going to go with
that huge bag of words--

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:51.934
instead of 20, now it's
1,000-- and pull similar cases.

00:29:51.934 --> 00:29:53.475
So that has three
distinct advantages

00:29:53.475 --> 00:29:56.610
that are pretty powerful.

00:29:56.610 --> 00:30:01.650
For starters, you can
now find historical cases

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.360
that don't intersect--
that have no words

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:07.020
at all intersecting with the
new incoming case, right?

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:09.430
Because you buffered that
with a new vocabulary,

00:30:09.430 --> 00:30:10.920
they could have
no word in common,

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:12.809
and you can still pull them out.

00:30:12.809 --> 00:30:14.850
The second one is there's
no training on our end.

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:16.350
We're not modeling
this on our end,

00:30:16.350 --> 00:30:19.000
because we're piggybacking on
the pre-trained word vectors,

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:19.500
right?

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.604
So all that work is pushed
on to somebody else.

00:30:21.604 --> 00:30:23.145
We don't have to do
any pre-training.

00:30:23.145 --> 00:30:25.180
That saves us a lot of time.

00:30:25.180 --> 00:30:27.300
And the third advantage,
which is related to this,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.792
is that because we're
not doing a pre-training,

00:30:29.792 --> 00:30:32.250
we don't have to waste any of
our own knowledge base data--

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:34.830
our own historical case
data-- for training.

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:37.530
We can then use
the entire corpus

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.969
to find similar complaints,
going all the way

00:30:39.969 --> 00:30:42.510
from the first complaint ever
entered in your data warehouse,

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.204
to the one that maybe it
was resolved 2 seconds ago.

00:30:45.204 --> 00:30:47.370
If it's similar, we can
find it, and we can pull it.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:49.855
So we have access to
your entire corpus.

00:30:49.855 --> 00:30:51.630
That's pretty powerful.

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:53.752
I'm going to do a demo as well.

00:30:53.752 --> 00:30:54.960
So I have a simple case here.

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:56.450
"I'm having issues with my bank.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:57.825
I have been late
on two payments,

00:30:57.825 --> 00:30:59.366
and I can't get in
touch with anybody

00:30:59.366 --> 00:31:01.170
to work on some kind
of repayment plan.

00:31:01.170 --> 00:31:04.390
I'll be upset if this ends
up hurting my credit score."

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:05.160
So I'll run this.

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:12.370
So the top of that, right?

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:15.280
So it's saying credit reporting,
which is correct, right?

00:31:15.280 --> 00:31:16.950
That sounds about right.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:18.460
The original case is there.

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:24.070
You have the information
from whom that came from,

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:25.730
and you have the
sentiment index.

00:31:25.730 --> 00:31:27.650
So more or less correct.

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:30.130
It's slightly
negative to neutral.

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:33.139
And the word cloud says
"issues with the bank,"

00:31:33.139 --> 00:31:34.180
and even the word "late."

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:38.599
So off the bat, you know
exactly what the case is.

00:31:38.599 --> 00:31:41.140
This becomes way more useful if
the case is very long, right?

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:44.350
Because you'll really
see things pop out.

00:31:44.350 --> 00:31:46.420
It says topic is finance.

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:48.890
And I purposely didn't
put a bank name in there,

00:31:48.890 --> 00:31:50.990
so it just says Bank
as the organization.

00:31:50.990 --> 00:31:52.124
So that's acceptable here.

00:31:52.124 --> 00:31:54.040
And then we see the
similar complaints, right?

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:56.519
So there are complaints
that are extremely long.

00:31:56.519 --> 00:31:58.060
And what I like here
is that it's not

00:31:58.060 --> 00:31:59.350
pulling anything too large.

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:04.600
So we know that it is looking
at not only the similar words,

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:06.510
but also weighing it correctly.

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:09.560
And we see everything is
related to a repayment plan.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:11.020
You see "can't repay my loan."

00:32:11.020 --> 00:32:12.790
"Can't repay my loan."

00:32:12.790 --> 00:32:14.950
"Dealing with my lender."

00:32:14.950 --> 00:32:16.480
"Need information
about my loan,"

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:18.370
or "struggling to
repay the loan."

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:21.535
And you'll see this
is not helping.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.080
Worried about their credit
score being affected.

00:32:32.080 --> 00:32:33.030
Here it is.

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:35.910
I picked one, here.

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:38.570
Late.

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:42.860
So I mean, they're all related
to the query in question,

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:43.610
right?

00:32:43.610 --> 00:32:46.430
And they're all very different,
but they're similar, right?

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:47.930
So that's what's
really interesting

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:48.890
about this approach.

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:54.370
And there's Word2vec
and there's Doc2vec.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.250
I don't know if you've
heard of these terms.

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:58.130
This is more paragraph
to vec, right?

00:32:58.130 --> 00:33:00.350
Maybe we'll make it
into a final product

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:02.990
where you can take a paragraph
and find a similar paragraph

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:04.700
by adding extra
weighting, by adding

00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:07.230
all these kind of things.

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:10.100
So I'll close this
by saying two things.

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:14.510
And I'm seeing the people
raising their hands.

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:16.370
I think we're all
on the same page.

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:18.406
There are two things
that are really great

00:33:18.406 --> 00:33:20.780
right now in natural language
processing-- in my opinion.

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:23.180
I've worked in natural
processing for the past 10

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:25.550
years, and I've seen
such a progression.

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:28.850
Two enormous boons to our work--

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:31.220
one is having access to
these pre-trained word

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:31.970
vectors, right?

00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:33.180
This is huge.

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:33.773
GloVe is one.

00:33:33.773 --> 00:33:35.150
There are tons of other ones.

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:36.950
You have them for
all major languages,

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:39.110
and you'll have them for
specialized languages--

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:40.880
legal, medical.

00:33:40.880 --> 00:33:43.820
People have gone
through the painstaking

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:47.660
work of modeling huge corpuses
of different languages

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:49.770
and made them available,
have released them

00:33:49.770 --> 00:33:51.270
in the public domain
for us to work.

00:33:51.270 --> 00:33:53.686
So I encourage anybody who
needs-- unless you need really,

00:33:53.686 --> 00:33:55.100
really specialized,
rare dialects

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:57.290
or rare technical
language, use them.

00:33:57.290 --> 00:34:00.360
It's probably better than
anything we can come up with.

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:02.650
These are usually PhD
students who work on it.

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:03.680
They do it really well.

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.500
We should leverage that work,
and it's there for us to use.

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:09.350
The second-- so Word2vec was
definitely a big one, right?

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:12.392
But the pre-trained word
vector's even better, right?

00:34:12.392 --> 00:34:14.100
Because you don't have
to do it yourself.

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:16.100
And the second thing that
was really a real boon

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:19.230
to natural language
processing is feeding language

00:34:19.230 --> 00:34:20.889
into convolutional
neural networks.

00:34:20.889 --> 00:34:24.409
Like Priya talked
about, CNNs seem

00:34:24.409 --> 00:34:26.179
to do anything these
days-- time series,

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:29.737
natural language processing,
along with images and sound,

00:34:29.737 --> 00:34:31.570
these kinds of things
that it does normally.

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:32.690
It does it really well.

00:34:32.690 --> 00:34:36.139
And a huge advantage is a lot
of the feature engineering

00:34:36.139 --> 00:34:38.580
is done automatically
by the neural network.

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:40.234
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:34:40.234 --> 00:34:41.650
And as we get
better at this, it's

00:34:41.650 --> 00:34:44.025
going to get a lot better than
any feature engineering we

00:34:44.025 --> 00:34:46.100
can come up with as humans.

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:46.940
We do have a GitHub.

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:49.075
It's not currently open.

00:34:49.075 --> 00:34:51.199
We were focusing on getting
this presentation done.

00:34:51.199 --> 00:34:52.574
We're going to
clean up the code,

00:34:52.574 --> 00:34:57.139
and then we'll publish
every one of these parts,

00:34:57.139 --> 00:35:02.000
but maybe in three separate
code bases in this GitHub

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:02.810
repository.

00:35:02.810 --> 00:35:04.310
If you want access,
please email us.

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:06.800
If you want access before
that, please email us.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:09.470
Also, the link to
the demo is there.

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:11.000
That also will be
open for a bit.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:13.220
We may close it after a while.

00:35:13.220 --> 00:35:15.890
And you have the
link to SpringML,

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:19.010
and Priya's and
my email address.

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.884
So if you have
questions about this--

00:35:21.884 --> 00:35:24.050
it doesn't have to be about
case-routing or anything

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.880
natural language processing,
or even anything CNNs--

00:35:25.880 --> 00:35:26.880
we'd love to help out.

00:35:26.880 --> 00:35:27.800
We'd love to get involved.

00:35:27.800 --> 00:35:29.383
If your organization
needs help there,

00:35:29.383 --> 00:35:31.008
we also would be
happy to get involved.

00:35:31.008 --> 00:35:32.799
So we're going to open
it up for questions.

00:35:32.799 --> 00:35:35.210
If there's any questions,
please, you can ask them.

00:35:35.210 --> 00:35:37.430
And we'll be around
afterwards as well.

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

